// Neorealism: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== neorealism.f01 | feature | episodic
title: Two Thousand Bricks
genre: neorealism + drama
tone: Dust-grey, level-eyed and stubbornly alive
era: 1945 | period
setting: A bombed capital in its first summer of peace | fictional | city
logline: In a city reduced to rubble, a woman who must clear two thousand bricks a day to keep the ration card that feeds her children discovers that someone is stealing the cleaned bricks from her stack at night.
premise: There are four hundred million cubic metres of rubble and few men. The occupying authority classes rations by work: the lowest card is called the death card; clearing rubble earns the second highest. A brick knocked clean of mortar with a hammer is worth a fraction of a pfennig and is counted at dusk. {lotte}, a tram conductor's widow with two children and a mother-in-law, joins a chain of women passing buckets.
synopsis: The film is a week, each day an episode, each built round a small practical problem. Monday: getting on the list, which requires a stamp from an office open two hours a day that has run out of ink. Tuesday: the work itself, shown at length, without comment: the chain, the hammers, the dust, the songs, a dud bomb found under a staircase and carried out in a pram. Wednesday: her count is three hundred short. Her stack has been raided in the night. The foreman, {herr_p}, who lost an arm and his certainty, can only write what he counts. Thursday: she sits up in the ruins to watch. The thief is a boy of ten, {the_boy}, who sells bricks for bread on the black market at the station; he lives in a cellar with a sister of four, and has no card at all, not being on any list. Friday: the dilemma, which the film will not make easy. Report him, and he goes to a camp for strays and the sister somewhere else. Say nothing, and her own children drop to the death card by month's end. She tries the offices: there is a form for orphans, requiring a death certificate for parents buried under a building nobody has yet dug out. Saturday: the women of the chain, who are not sentimental, argue it over at the midday soup; {frau_k}, a professor's widow with ruined hands, does the arithmetic: thirty women, ten bricks each, is a boy's quota. They put him in the chain, small enough to crawl where they cannot, and write him on the list under a dead husband's surname. Sunday: nothing is solved. {herr_p} looks at the name, and at the boy, and at the ink, and counts. The mother-in-law, who has said all week that charity is for people with coal, cuts the bread into six instead of four. In the last shot the chain is at work on a new street, thirty-one long, and the camera rises just enough to show how many streets there are.
question: How much can people with nothing afford to give?
themes: survival as shared arithmetic; bureaucracy after the catastrophe; women rebuilding what men destroyed
audience: Adults who like their films plain and true
viewpoint: With {lotte}, at eye level, with non-professional faces around her
stakes: Her children's rations, a boy's and a small girl's lives
conflict: A mother's duty to her own against a stray child who is robbing her to live, and both against the forms
ending: The women take the boy into the chain under a false name. The foreman lets it pass. Nothing else is fixed.
experience: The texture of labour, anger without a villain, and a small defiant warmth
look: Real ruins in flat daylight; headscarves, aprons, hammers; chalk tallies on a wall; dust in every frame
sound: Hammers on brick, hundreds of them, out of time. Buckets. A song started and dropped.
m: survive | system | self-vs-others | open
@ lotte | Lotte Brandis | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 34 | The second card, kept | Two children and an old woman eat by it | A child is stealing it from her | Tired, fair-minded, short-tempered, incapable of looking away | From guarding her stack to sharing it | Dialect, clipped, tired jokes | Headscarf, a man's jacket, a brick hammer
@ the_boy | The Boy, called Fritz | co-lead | foil | human | he/him | 10 | Bread for his sister today | Nobody else is going to | He is on no list and so does not exist | Feral, quick, absolutely loyal | From thief to the smallest link in the chain | Says almost nothing; lies fluently | A soldier's cap, bare feet, a handcart
@ herr_p | Herr Pohl | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 50 | A count that will pass inspection | It is the last order he has | The count is short, then oddly long | Correct, hollowed, decent underneath | From counting bricks to not looking at a name | Numbers, regulations | One sleeve pinned up, a tally board
@ frau_k | Frau Doctor Kessler | supporting | mentor | human | she/her | 61 | To be useful, and not pitied | Her library is under the bricks she is cleaning | Her hands | Dry, exact, unexpectedly funny | From keeping apart to doing the sum for everyone | Educated, ironic | A good coat worn to work, ruined gloves
@ oma | Oma Brandis | supporting | foil | human | she/her | 70 | Her grandchildren fed first | She has buried a son | Another mouth | Hard, frightened, not without a heart | From "charity is for people with coal" to cutting six slices | Proverbs, complaints | Black dress, a bread knife
~ lotte - the_boy | robbed and robber | a watch kept at night | a name lent
~ lotte - herr_p | worker and foreman | a short count | a long look and a tally
~ lotte - frau_k | two women in the chain | nods | an argument won with arithmetic
~ lotte - oma | daughter-in-law and mother-in-law | four slices | six
# Monday | INT | Labour Office | DAY | lotte | Get the stamp | A queue round a ruin. A window open two hours. No ink. She waits for the ink. She gets the card with her name spelled wrong | | 5 | crowd=150, props=ration card
# Tuesday | EXT | Rubble Street | DAY | lotte, frau_k, herr_p | Work | The chain. Buckets hand to hand. Hammers. Dust. A dud bomb under a staircase, carried out in a pram at walking pace while thirty women hold their breath | A day's work is shown | 8 | crowd=60, stunts=unexploded bomb carried, props=buckets+hammers+pram
# Wednesday | EXT | Rubble Street, Brick Stacks | DUSK | lotte, herr_p | Come up short | The count. Three hundred under. Her stack has a bite out of it. He can only write what is there | Her bricks are being stolen | 5 | props=tally board
# Thursday | EXT | Rubble Street | NIGHT | lotte, the_boy | Catch him | Moonlight. A handcart. A boy stacking her bricks with care. She follows him to a cellar: a mattress, a girl of four, a pan | The thief is a child | 7 | props=handcart
# Friday | INT | Welfare Office | DAY | lotte | Ask for a form | There is a form. It needs the parents' death certificates. The parents are under a building. The building is on next year's list | | 5 | crowd=80
# Saturday | EXT | Rubble Street, Soup Kettle | DAY | lotte, frau_k, the_boy | Do the sum | Midday. An argument: she has her own. {frau_k} says thirty times ten is three hundred. Silence, spoons. Someone hands the boy a hammer | The chain takes him in | 6 | crowd=40, props=soup kettle
# Sunday | EXT | Rubble Street | DAY | herr_p, lotte, the_boy | Be counted | A new name on the list, a dead man's surname. He looks at it, at the boy, at his pencil. He counts | | 5 | crowd=40, props=tally board
# Sunday | INT | Cellar Kitchen | NIGHT | lotte, oma, the_boy | Cut the bread | One loaf. The old woman takes the knife, measures with her thumb, and cuts six | | 4 | props=bread
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the women who cleared the rubble of bombed cities in 1945, with invented people
